1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,190
OneFit Engine fits models
to experimental data.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,961
It comes in two parts that
work together: OFE-Go,

3
00:00:07,961 --> 00:00:10,177
the command line, and OFE-GUI,

4
00:00:10,177 --> 00:00:12,965
an interface in your web browser.

5
00:00:13,115 --> 00:00:17,847
Here is a short tour, following one real
measurement from the data to the report.

6
00:00:19,347 --> 00:00:22,967
With OFE-Go, a fit is a single command.

7
00:00:23,117 --> 00:00:25,311
You give it a model expression,

8
00:00:25,311 --> 00:00:29,203
with starting values and
limits, and a data file: here,

9
00:00:29,203 --> 00:00:32,172
a field-cycling NMR relaxation curve.

10
00:00:32,322 --> 00:00:35,432
The engine compiles the
model, minimises chi-square,

11
00:00:35,432 --> 00:00:38,295
and prints every parameter with its error.

12
00:00:38,445 --> 00:00:44,521
T1 comes out at 2.00, plus or minus 0.03.

13
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:47,568
The same command line
also creates engine files,

14
00:00:47,568 --> 00:00:49,921
plots and converts data, archives work,

15
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:52,685
and checks the installation with doctor.

16
00:00:54,185 --> 00:00:56,303
There are two ways to start a fit.

17
00:00:56,453 --> 00:01:00,509
Give it a model expression and
data files, as we just did,

18
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:04,636
or load a saved fit description
in JSON or SAV format.

19
00:01:04,786 --> 00:01:08,904
A jobs file runs many fit
descriptions as one batch.

20
00:01:10,404 --> 00:01:13,051
Then choose how the parameters are shared.

21
00:01:13,201 --> 00:01:16,496
Individual fits each data set on its own.

22
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:19,995
Global shares parameters
across all the data sets.

23
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:24,584
Hybrid shares the ones you choose,
and fits the rest per data set.

24
00:01:26,084 --> 00:01:29,508
OFE-GUI runs the same fit in the browser.

25
00:01:29,658 --> 00:01:32,997
On the left are the model
expression and the data file.

26
00:01:33,147 --> 00:01:36,678
The options map directly to
command-line flags: here,

27
00:01:36,678 --> 00:01:40,160
a logarithmic time axis
and reduced chi-square.

28
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:45,134
In the centre, the Terminal is an ordinary
shell on the machine that runs the fits.

29
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:49,774
You can also start an optional,
separately installed AI assistant there.

30
00:01:49,924 --> 00:01:54,824
Below it, in Plots, the fitted curve
follows the data across the whole decay.

31
00:01:56,324 --> 00:02:00,708
In OFE-Figures, the fit plot
becomes a publication figure.

32
00:02:00,858 --> 00:02:04,623
I added a title, and a
subtitle with the fitted T1.

33
00:02:04,773 --> 00:02:08,243
The axis labels use a
Greek tau and a subscript.

34
00:02:08,393 --> 00:02:15,816
plot-go draws it, and exports
PDF, EPS, SVG, PNG or TIFF.

35
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:21,114
OFE-Report turns the
finished run into a report.

36
00:02:21,264 --> 00:02:24,662
It records the engine, the
exact command and the model,

37
00:02:24,662 --> 00:02:28,471
and checks every result file
against the run's manifest.

38
00:02:28,621 --> 00:02:36,689
Results are rounded for publication:
T1 is 1.998, plus or minus 0.030.

39
00:02:36,839 --> 00:02:39,336
The edited figure is included.

40
00:02:39,486 --> 00:02:45,529
Save it, print it, or download it
as PDF, Word, Excel or Markdown.

41
00:02:47,029 --> 00:02:51,001
Prepare, fit, inspect, and communicate.

42
00:02:51,151 --> 00:02:53,535
Use OFE-GUI on your own computer,

43
00:02:53,535 --> 00:02:56,208
or connect it to a remote Linux host,

44
00:02:56,208 --> 00:02:59,592
and use OFE-Go for scripts and automation.

45
00:02:59,742 --> 00:03:06,468
Both OFE-Go and OFE-GUI are currently
distributed under the Artistic License 2.0.

46
00:03:06,618 --> 00:03:11,188
A separate commercial license for
eligible project code is in preparation.
